person-group><article-title>Cells lying on a bed of microneedles: an approach to isolate mechanical force</article-title><source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA</source><volume>100</volume><year>2003</year><fpage>1484</fpage><lpage>1489</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1073/pnas.0235407100">doi:10.1073/pnas.0235407100</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12552122</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="bib48"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Th&#x000e9;ry</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>P&#x000e9;pin</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Dressaire</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Bornens</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cell distribution of stress fibres in response to the geometry of the adhesive environment</article-title><source>Cell Motil. Cytoskel</source><volume>63</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>341</fpage><lpage>355</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1002/cm.20126">doi:10.1002/cm.20126</ext-link></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="bib49"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Butler</surname><given-names>J.P</given-names></name><name><surname>Ingber</surname><given-names>D.E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mechanotransduction across the cell surface and through the cytoskeleton</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>260</volume><year>1993</year><fpage>1124</fpage><lpage>1127</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1126/science.7684161">doi:10.1126/science.7684161</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7684161</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="bib50"><mixed-citation publication-type="other">Wei, Z., Deshpande, V. S., McMeeking, R. M. &#x00026; Evans, A. G. In press. Analysis and interpretation of stress fiber organization in cells subject to cyclic stretch. <italic>J. Biomech. Eng. ASME</italic></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="bib51"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yeung</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Effects of substrate stiffness on cell morphology, cytoskeletal structure, and adhesion</article-title><source>Cell Motil. Cytoskel</source><volume>60</volume><year>2005</year><fpage>24</fpage><lpage>34</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1002/cm.20041">doi:10.1002/cm.20041</ext-link></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Experimental images of average (<italic>a</italic>) actin and (<italic>b</italic>) vinculin distributions for hTERT-RPE1 cells plated on V-, T-, Y- and U-shaped ligand micro-patterns. The crosses in (<italic>a</italic>) indicate the endpoints and location of maximum displacement of the non-adhered cell edges used in estimating the radii of curvatures of those cell edges. The length of the triangular edge is 46&#x0200a;&#x003bc;m and a scale bar is included for reference purposes. Reproduced with some modifications from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib48">Th&#x000e9;ry <italic>et al</italic>. (2006)</xref>. Copyright &#x000a9; John Wiley &#x00026; Sons, Ltd.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f01"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Schematic of a cell on a ligand-coated substrate. The network of stress fibres and the integrin&#x02013;ligand complex part of the FA are shown as insets.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f02"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Predictions of the distributions of the stress-fibre and integrin concentrations along the radius of a circular cell of radius <italic>R</italic>=17.5&#x0200a;&#x003bc;m on a circular ligand patch of equal radius. The distributions are shown at five selected times. In (<italic>a</italic>) the stress-fibre concentrations as parametrized by <italic>&#x00393;</italic> are plotted while (<italic>b</italic>,<italic>c</italic>) show the normalized high, <italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>H</sub>/<italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>0</sub>, and low, <italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>L</sub>/<italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>0</sub>, affinity integrin concentrations, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f03"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Predictions of the relationship between the stress-fibre concentration, <italic>&#x00393;</italic>, and the high-affinity integrin concentration, <italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>H</sub>/<italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>0</sub>, at two locations, <italic>r</italic>, near the periphery of the circular cell of radius <italic>R</italic>=17.5&#x0200a;&#x003bc;m on a circular ligand patch of equal radius: (<italic>a</italic>) <italic>r</italic>/<italic>R</italic>=0.95 and (<italic>b</italic>) <italic>r</italic>/<italic>R</italic>=0.9. The parameter <italic>&#x00393;</italic> increases monotonically with time and the selected normalized times <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic> are marked adjacent to the square symbols.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f04"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Predictions of the steady-state distributions of the stress-fibre and integrin concentrations for a triangular cell on a ligand pattern of the same shape. (<italic>a</italic>) Sketch of triangular pattern (all dimensions are in micrometres). Steady-state distributions of (<italic>b</italic>) <italic>&#x00393;</italic> and (<italic>c</italic>) <italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>H</sub>/<italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>0</sub>. In (<italic>b</italic>) the direction <italic>&#x003d5;</italic><sub>S</sub> is indicated by line segments having length scaled by <italic>&#x00393;</italic>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f05"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Predictions of the stress-fibre distributions in a circular cell of radius <italic>R</italic>=17.5&#x0200a;&#x003bc;m on a circular ligand patch of equal radius. Steady-state distributions of <italic>&#x00393;</italic> (<italic>a</italic>) after application of an anisotropic activation signal that constrains stress-fibre formation to <inline-formula><alternatives><mml:math id="M1"><mml:mrow><mml:mo>|</mml:mo><mml:mi>&#x003d5;</mml:mi><mml:mo>|</mml:mo><mml:mo>&#x02264;</mml:mo><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mo>&#x000b0;</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math><inline-graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182e01.jpg"/></alternatives></inline-formula> and (<italic>b</italic>) after a second isotropic activation signal is applied to the cell with the initially anisotropic stress-fibre distribution shown in (<italic>a</italic>). (<italic>c</italic>) The control case of the steady-state distributions of <italic>&#x00393;</italic> after the application of two isotropic activation signals. The direction <italic>&#x003d5;</italic><sub>S</sub> is indicated by line segments having length scaled by <italic>&#x00393;</italic> in all cases.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f06"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Predictions of the steady-state FA distributions as parametrized by <italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>H</sub>/<italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>0</sub> in a circular cell of radius <italic>R</italic>=17.5&#x0200a;&#x003bc;m. The FA distributions in (<italic>a</italic>) and (<italic>b</italic>) correspond to the stress-fibre distributions in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig6">figure 6</xref><italic>b</italic> and <italic>c</italic>, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f07"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Sketches of ligand pattern and cell shape for the (<italic>a</italic>) V-, (<italic>b</italic>) T-, (<italic>c</italic>) Y- and (<italic>d</italic>) U-shaped ligand patterns. The endpoints of the non-adhered cell edges are marked by crosses and all dimensions are in micrometres.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f08"/></fig><fig id="fig9" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Predictions of the distributions of the stress fibres as parametrized by <italic>&#x00393;</italic> for a cell on the V-shaped ligand pattern. The predictions are shown at four selected times (<italic>a</italic>, <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic>=2.1; <italic>b</italic>, <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic>=3.97; <italic>c</italic>, <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic>=7.85; <italic>d</italic>, <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic>=15) and the direction <italic>&#x003d5;</italic><sub>S</sub> is indicated by line segments whose length is scaled by <italic>&#x00393;</italic> in all cases.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f09"/></fig><fig id="fig10" position="float"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Predictions of the distributions of vinculin or FA concentration as parametrized by <italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>H</sub>/<italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>0</sub> for a cell on the V-shaped ligand pattern. The predictions are shown at four selected times (<italic>a</italic>, <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic>=0.22; <italic>b</italic>, <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic>=0.35; <italic>c</italic>, <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic>=0.72; <italic>d</italic>, <italic>t</italic>/<italic>&#x003b8;</italic>=2.1).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f10"/></fig><fig id="fig11" position="float"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>Predictions of the time evolution of the radius of curvature, <italic>&#x003bb;</italic>, of the non-adhered edge of the cell on the V-shaped ligand pattern. Predictions are shown for three values of <inline-formula><alternatives><mml:math id="M2"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mover accent="true"><mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#x000af;</mml:mo></mml:mover><mml:mtext>v</mml:mtext></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:math><inline-graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182e02.jpg"/></alternatives></inline-formula> (including the reference value, <inline-formula><alternatives><mml:math id="M3"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mover accent="true"><mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#x000af;</mml:mo></mml:mover><mml:mtext>v</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>6</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math><inline-graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182e03.jpg"/></alternatives></inline-formula>).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f11"/></fig><fig id="fig12" position="float"><label>Figure 12</label><caption><p>Predictions of the steady-state distribution of actin as parametrized by <italic>&#x00393;</italic> for the four concave ligand patterns investigated here and sketched in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig8">figure 8</xref>. The direction <italic>&#x003d5;</italic><sub>S</sub> is indicated by line segments whose length is scaled by <italic>&#x00393;</italic> in all cases.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f12"/></fig><fig id="fig13" position="float"><label>Figure 13</label><caption><p>Predictions of the steady-state distribution of vinculin as parametrized by <italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>H</sub>/<italic>&#x003be;</italic><sub>0</sub> for the four concave ligand patterns investigated here and sketched in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig8">figure 8</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f13"/></fig><fig id="fig14" position="float"><label>Figure 14</label><caption><p>(<italic>a</italic>) A comparison of the measured and predicted steady-state radii of curvature, <italic>&#x003bb;</italic><sub>ss</sub>, of the non-adhered edges of the cells on four concave ligand patterns. The predictions employ the reference value, <inline-formula><alternatives><mml:math id="M4"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mover accent="true"><mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#x000af;</mml:mo></mml:mover><mml:mtext>v</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>6</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math><inline-graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182e04.jpg"/></alternatives></inline-formula>. (<italic>b</italic>) The predictions of <italic>&#x003bb;</italic><sub>ss</sub> for three selected values of <inline-formula><alternatives><mml:math id="M5"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mover accent="true"><mml:mi>k</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#x000af;</mml:mo></mml:mover><mml:mtext>v</mml:mtext></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:math><inline-graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182e05.jpg"/></alternatives></inline-formula> including the reference value.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f14"/></fig><fig id="fig15" position="float"><label>Figure 15</label><caption><p>The steady-state distributions of the normalized maximum principal stress, <italic>&#x003c3;</italic><sub>p</sub>/<italic>&#x003c3;</italic><sub>max</sub>, in the cells on the four concave ligand patterns. The direction of the maximum principal stress is indicated by line segments whose length is scaled by <italic>&#x00393;</italic>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="rsif20071182f15"/></fig></floats-group></article>